#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.65pt, 779.4pt, "3 ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 88.58pt, "residual mismatch between the model and the data). Yang ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(381.31pt, 88.58pt, "et al")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(403.39pt, 88.58pt, ". [22] later derived a ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 113.06pt, "more complete analytical formulation that explicitly incorporated both parameter ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 137.42pt, "correlations through the full covariance matrix and the contribution of fitting residuals. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 161.9pt, "While this advanced the state of the art, the analytical route still relies fundamentally ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 186.38pt, "on local linearization and on the fitted variables being approximately Gaussian. When ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 210.86pt, "parameter sensitivities are weak, strongly coupled, or markedly nonlinear, these ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 235.25pt, "assumptions may break down, and the analytical results can become inaccurate. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 259.73pt, "Monte Carlo analysis is often regarded as a more general alternative because it ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 284.21pt, "propagates uncertainty through the full nonlinear fitting procedure and can naturally ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 308.69pt, "capture parameter coupling and non-Gaussian output distributions. This generality, ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 333.05pt, "however, comes at a much higher computational cost. More importantly, its accuracy ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 357.53pt, "still depends on whether the fitting algorithm can consistently converge to the global ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 382.01pt, "optimum in every trial: improper scaling, unfavorable initial guesses, and convergence ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 406.51pt, "to local minima can significantly distort the inferred parameter distributions and thereby ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 430.87pt, "compromise the uncertainty estimate [23-25]. Recent efforts to mitigate these ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 455.35pt, "robustness issues include deep-learning-assisted fitting to provide better initial guesses ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 479.83pt, "[23] and Bayesian parameter estimation (BPE) to unify fitting and uncertainty ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 504.31pt, "quantification in a probabilistic framework [25]. While BPE can explicitly account for ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 528.67pt, "multivariable correlations and prior knowledge, it generally requires a much broader ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 553.18pt, "exploration of parameter space and remains computationally expensive for multilayer ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 577.66pt, "systems with several coupled unknowns. Likewise, machine-learning-based ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 602.14pt, "approaches can accelerate inference but typically require large training datasets and ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 626.5pt, "may lose accuracy outside the training domain. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 650.98pt, "Existing methods thus involve clear trade-offs between efficiency, generality, ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 675.46pt, "robustness, and interpretability. Analytical methods are fast but rely on strong local ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 699.96pt, "assumptions; MC methods are more general but computationally demanding and ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 724.32pt, "sensitive to fitting robustness; Bayesian and machine-learning approaches offer ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 748.8pt, "rigorous or accelerated alternatives but impose their own computational or data ")
]

// arXiv source page 4
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.65pt, 779.4pt, "4 ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 88.58pt, "requirements. Against this background, two key methodological questions remain ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 113.06pt, "unresolved. First, how can we evaluate uncertainty both efficiently and accurately when ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 137.42pt, "fitted parameter distributions deviate substantially from Gaussian behavior? Second, ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 161.9pt, "under what conditions are Monte Carlo-inferred parameter distributions truly reliable? ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 186.38pt, "In this work, we address both challenges through complementary advances in ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 210.86pt, "Monte Carlo sampling and analytical uncertainty propagation. We first establish that ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 235.25pt, "parameter normalization, i.e., fitting dimensionless ratios relative to their optimal ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 259.73pt, "estimates, is a practical prerequisite for trustworthy MC uncertainty estimation when ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 284.21pt, "fitted parameters span vastly different magnitudes. We further demonstrate that ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 308.69pt, "thermoreflectance inverse problems possess an intrinsic statistical geometry rooted in ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 333.05pt, "the multilayer heat-diffusion equation. This geometry defines natural fitting coordinates ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 357.53pt, "where parameter distributions become approximately Gaussian, allowing analytical ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 382.01pt, "uncertainty propagation to remain accurate well beyond the locally linear regime. The ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 406.51pt, "resulting framework is benchmarked against normalized MC simulations across ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 430.87pt, "representative ")
  #line-at(171.36pt, 430.87pt, "multilayer ")
  #line-at(234.912pt, 430.87pt, "systems ")
  #line-at(287.208pt, 430.87pt, "and ")
  #line-at(318.636pt, 430.87pt, "further ")
  #line-at(365.52pt, 430.87pt, "validated ")
  #line-at(423.684pt, 430.87pt, "using ")
  #line-at(463.86pt, 430.87pt, "different ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 455.35pt, "thermoreflectance modalities. These cases encompass (i) metal-dielectric interfaces ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 479.83pt, "with weak interfacial sensitivity, (ii) anisotropic substrates where the in-plane and ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 504.31pt, "cross-plane conductivities are strongly coupled, and (iii) multilayer thin films with ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 528.67pt, "simultaneous uncertainty in thickness, thermal conductivity, and volumetric heat ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 553.18pt, "capacity. Close agreement between the transformed analytical and MC results is ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 577.66pt, "obtained in all cases, confirming that the analytical route retains the statistical fidelity ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 602.14pt, "of the MC approach at a fraction of the computational cost. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90.024pt, 645.58pt, "2. METHODOLOGIES ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90.024pt, 671.74pt, "2.1 Inverse problem formulation ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(114.02pt, 696.12pt, "Thermoreflectance experiments probe the thermal response of multilayer ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 720.6pt, "structures under periodic optical heating. As illustrated in Fig. 1(a), the sample is ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 745.08pt, "modeled as a multilayer stack consisting of a metal transducer, one or more thin films, ")
]

