  #line-at(498.76pt, 137.54pt, " . ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 161.9pt, "Throughout this work, we use the term “natural coordinates” to denote the parameter ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 186.38pt, "coordinates in which the fitted distributions are approximately Gaussian. As shown in ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 210.86pt, "the next section, these coordinates are not the original physical parameters themselves ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 235.25pt, "but specific reciprocal, square-root, or composite combinations of them, emerging from ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 259.73pt, "the structural form of the multilayer heat-diffusion equation. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90.024pt, 304.61pt, "2.4 Physics-motivated variable transformation ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(114.02pt, 325.01pt, "The transformation anticipated in Section 2.3 follows directly from the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 349.37pt, "mathematical structure of the multilayer heat-diffusion problem. Rather than ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 373.85pt, "postulating these coordinates empirically, we derive them from the governing equation ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 398.35pt, "to establish an a priori expectation for the natural statistical coordinates of the inverse ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 422.83pt, "problem. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 447.19pt, "Consider first the interfacial thermal conductance. Equation (3) shows that, for a ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 471.79pt, "given interfacial heat flux ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(227.81pt, 471.79pt, "𝑞")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(235.73pt, 471.79pt, ", the temperature discontinuity across the interface is ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 496.27pt, "strictly linear in the interfacial thermal resistance ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(335.83pt, 496.27pt, "1/𝐺")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(356.47pt, 496.27pt, ". Because thermoreflectance is ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 520.63pt, "fundamentally a thermometry technique that measures temperature response rather than ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 545.14pt, "heat flux directly, ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(185.42pt, 545.14pt, "1/𝐺")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(206.69pt, 545.14pt, " is the quantity that enters the boundary condition linearly. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 569.62pt, "Consequently, from the perspective of the temperature field, ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(388.03pt, 569.62pt, "1/𝐺")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(408.67pt, 569.62pt, " is the more natural ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 594.1pt, "variable than ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(158.3pt, 594.1pt, "𝐺")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(166.34pt, 594.1pt, " itself. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 618.58pt, "For the substrate, we apply the Fourier transform to the time variable ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(458.74pt, 618.58pt, "𝑡")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(463.78pt, 618.58pt, " and the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 642.94pt, "Hankel transform to the radial coordinate ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(298.49pt, 642.94pt, "𝑟")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(304.49pt, 642.94pt, " in Eq. (2). The governing equation then ")
