  #line-at(114.02pt, 88.58pt, "To reduce the risk of convergence to local optima across thousands of MC trials, ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 113.06pt, "a hybrid optimization strategy [27] is adopted. A particle swarm optimization (PSO) ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 137.42pt, "algorithm first searches the parameter space globally with a swarm size of 200 and 20 ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 161.9pt, "generations. Once the global optimum region is identified, a quasi-Newton local ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 186.38pt, "optimizer refines the solution. This strategy is far less sensitive to the initial guess than ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 210.86pt, "conventional local optimization alone, because the first PSO generation already ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 235.25pt, "provides broad coverage of the admissible parameter space. Broad fitting bounds were ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 259.73pt, "used for all unknown parameters to minimize the influence of parameter constraints on ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 284.21pt, "the resulting MC distributions. Depending on parameter sensitivity, the bounds were ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 308.69pt, "set to 0.1–10, 0.2–5, or 0.5–2 times the corresponding nominal values, all of which ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 333.05pt, "extend well beyond the ranges sampled by the resulting MC distributions. Under the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 357.53pt, "computational settings used here, one MC trial required approximately 3 s on average. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 382.01pt, "Figure 4 compares the MC distributions obtained with and without parameter ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 406.51pt, "normalization. Figures 4(a–e) show the results obtained by directly fitting the original ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 430.15pt, "dimensional parameters. Only ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(239.93pt, 430.15pt, "𝐺")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(247.13pt, 432.55pt, "Al/GaN")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(277.37pt, 430.15pt, " exhibits an approximately normal distribution, ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 455.35pt, "whereas the other four parameters show pronounced skewness and substantially ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 479.83pt, "broader distributions. By contrast, Figs. 4(f–j) show the recovered distributions ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 504.31pt, "obtained from normalized fitting, as defined in Section 2.2. In principle, because ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 528.67pt, "normalization is a one-to-one reparameterization, the two approaches should yield ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 553.18pt, "identical results in the physical parameter space if every fit converges accurately to the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 577.66pt, "same global optimum. A trial-by-trial comparison of the final objective-function values ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 602.14pt, "further shows that normalized fitting yields lower values in all MC trials, while the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 626.5pt, "corresponding root-mean-square fitting residuals are comparable to the experimental ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 650.98pt, "noise level (Section S1, Supplementary Information). The observed discrepancy ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 675.46pt, "therefore reflects numerical optimization effects. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 699.96pt, "Because the fitted parameters span several orders of magnitude, direct fitting in ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 724.32pt, "the original dimensional parameter space produces a poorly scaled optimization ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 748.8pt, "landscape, for which convergence tolerances appropriate for one parameter may be ")
]

