#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(291.65pt, 779.4pt, "30 ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 88.58pt, "The consistency observed in Table 1 has direct practical implications. Once the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 113.06pt, "natural coordinates are identified, the analytical method provides a computationally ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 137.42pt, "efficient alternative to full Monte Carlo analysis without sacrificing statistical fidelity. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 161.9pt, "For a typical multilayer thermoreflectance fit involving three to six unknowns and ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 186.38pt, "multiple datasets, the analytical route reduces the uncertainty-evaluation time from ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 210.86pt, "repeated nonlinear optimizations to a single evaluation of the Jacobian and covariance ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 235.25pt, "matrices (approximately 0.6 s). The complete uncertainty evaluation requires a ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 259.73pt, "computational time comparable to that of a single MC refit. In contrast, full MC ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 284.21pt, "uncertainty propagation requires thousands of such nonlinear refits, resulting in orders-")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 308.69pt, "of-magnitude greater computational cost. The substantial efficiency gain arises because ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 333.05pt, "the transformation identifies the coordinates in which the inverse problem is locally ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 357.53pt, "closer to linear and the fitted distributions are closer to Gaussian. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 382.01pt, "Equations (23) and (24), together with the joint-sampling reconstruction described ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 406.51pt, "above, make the asymmetric uncertainty of every recovered physical parameter ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 430.87pt, "available analytically at negligible additional cost. Table 2 compares these analytical ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 455.35pt, "bounds with the central 95.45% intervals read directly from the full Monte Carlo ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 479.83pt, "distributions for all recovered single parameters, spanning the interfacial, substrate, and ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 504.31pt, "thin-film categories. In every case, the analytical method recovers the correct direction ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 528.67pt, "of the asymmetry, typically with a larger positive than negative deviation. The ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 553.18pt, "magnitudes also agree closely: averaged over the recovered parameters, the lower ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 577.66pt, "bounds differ by only a few percentage points, while the upper bounds show larger but ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 602.14pt, "still acceptable deviations. The largest discrepancies occur for strongly right-skewed ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 626.5pt, "interfacial conductances, such as ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(249.29pt, 626.5pt, "G")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(257.93pt, 627.46pt, "Al/silica")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(285.17pt, 626.5pt, " and the weakly sensitive ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(407.47pt, 626.5pt, "G")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(416.11pt, 627.46pt, "Al/SiO2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(442.99pt, 626.5pt, " of the 2-μm-")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 650.98pt, "film case, whose extreme upper tails are governed by residual non-Gaussianity and are ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 675.46pt, "therefore the most sensitive to finite-sample noise. Even in these cases, however, the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 699.96pt, "transformed analytical interval is far more faithful than a symmetric ±2σ interval ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 724.32pt, "evaluated directly in the original conductance coordinate, which would underestimate ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 748.8pt, "the positive tail and overestimate the negative one.  ")
]

