// arXiv source page 33
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-33.png", width: 418.5pt), 88.5pt, 72pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(291.65pt, 779.4pt, "33 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(112.1pt, 333.05pt, "FIG. 11. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(157.46pt, 333.05pt, "Workflow for practitioners to select natural coordinates and recover ")
  #line-at(240.05pt, 357.53pt, "parameter uncertainties. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 390.19pt, "The practical relevance of the proposed framework can be illustrated by several ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 414.67pt, "engineering scenarios. In AlGaN/GaN power-electronic heterostructures, the thermal ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 439.03pt, "conductivities of the constituent layers and interfacial thermal resistances are important ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 463.51pt, "inputs for device-level thermal simulations [31], and their uncertainties therefore affect ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 487.99pt, "the confidence of predicted operating temperatures. For anisotropic wide-bandgap ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 512.47pt, "semiconductors such as β-Ga")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(238.13pt, 513.43pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(242.21pt, 512.47pt, "O")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(250.85pt, 513.43pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(254.93pt, 512.47pt, ", directional thermal conductivity is an important ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 536.83pt, "consideration in high-power thermal management [32]; the substrate natural ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 561.34pt, "coordinates identified here provide a physically motivated basis for quantifying the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 585.82pt, "coupled uncertainty of in-plane and cross-plane thermal transport properties. In three-")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 610.3pt, "dimensional heterogeneous integration, FDTR has been used to characterize the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 634.66pt, "thermal resistance of fusion-bonded Si/SiO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(298.61pt, 635.62pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(302.69pt, 634.66pt, " stacks, where buried dielectric layers and ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 659.14pt, "interfaces can form important thermal bottlenecks [33]. The interface and thin-film ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 683.62pt, "natural coordinates developed here can provide a computationally efficient route for ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 708.12pt, "quantifying uncertainty in such fitted thermal properties. These examples illustrate how ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 732.48pt, "the proposed framework can support thermal characterization for device modeling, ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 756.96pt, "material and process comparison, and multilayer thermal-design decisions. ")
]

// arXiv source page 34
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-34.png", width: 418.5pt), 88.5pt, 72pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(291.65pt, 779.4pt, "34 ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 88.58pt, "Several limitations of the present framework should nevertheless be emphasized. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 113.06pt, "First, the natural coordinates derived here make the fitted distributions approximately ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 137.42pt, "Gaussian, but they do not provide a mathematical guarantee of exact normality. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 161.9pt, "Residual skewness can remain when a parameter has extremely weak sensitivity, when ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 186.38pt, "the posterior distribution is strongly truncated by parameter bounds, or when multiple ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 210.86pt, "local minima contribute to the fit. In such cases, the transformed analytical method ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 235.25pt, "should be checked against normalized Monte Carlo sampling or Bayesian posterior ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 259.73pt, "sampling. Second, the covariance formulation is still a local method: it assumes that the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 284.21pt, "inverse problem can be adequately linearized near the optimum in the transformed ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 308.69pt, "space. If the sensitivity matrix is nearly singular or if the fitted parameters are so ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 333.05pt, "strongly coupled that the local covariance ellipsoid no longer represents the global ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 357.53pt, "uncertainty structure, full probabilistic sampling remains necessary. Third, the present ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 382.01pt, "demonstrations assume that the known input parameters can be represented by ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 406.51pt, "independent Gaussian uncertainties. Correlated systematic uncertainties, such as shared ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 430.87pt, "errors in film thickness, heat capacity, transducer properties, or laser spot size, can in ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 455.35pt, "principle be incorporated through a non-diagonal input covariance matrix, but this ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 479.83pt, "extension has not been systematically examined here. Finally, the proposed coordinates ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 504.31pt, "are derived from the diffusive multilayer heat-conduction equation. They are therefore ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 528.67pt, "not expected to apply unchanged to regimes where the governing physics differs ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 553.18pt, "fundamentally, such as ballistic, quasi-ballistic, hydrodynamic, or strongly non-Fourier ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 577.66pt, "heat transport. ")
  #line-at(114.02pt, 602.14pt, "Within these boundaries, the workflow is expected to apply to thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 626.5pt, "techniques that share the same multilayer heat-diffusion kernel (Eq. (2)), including ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 650.98pt, "TDTR, FDTR, SDTR, SSTR, and SPS. For techniques or materials governed by ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 675.46pt, "fundamentally different transport equations, an analogous derivation should be ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 699.96pt, "performed from the corresponding governing model before applying covariance-based ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 724.32pt, "propagation. ")
]

