  #line-at(114.02pt, 88.58pt, "When such coordinates are not obvious from the governing equations, the present ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 113.06pt, "framework also suggests a possible data-driven extension. The physics-derived ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 137.42pt, "transformations used here are conceptually related to the change-of-variables principle ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 161.9pt, "underlying normalizing flows [34, 35], where a non-Gaussian distribution is mapped ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 186.38pt, "into a simpler latent space. In future work, invertible mappings trained on Monte Carlo ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 210.86pt, "or Bayesian posterior samples could be used to identify approximately Gaussian ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 235.25pt, "coordinates for more complex inverse problems. However, such learned ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 259.73pt, "transformations would generally be numerical rather than closed-form, and would ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 284.21pt, "require ")
  #line-at(138.132pt, 284.21pt, "dimensional ")
  #line-at(211.056pt, 284.21pt, "constraints, ")
  #line-at(280.37pt, 284.21pt, "cross-geometry ")
  #line-at(368.546pt, 284.21pt, "validation, ")
  #line-at(433.858pt, 284.21pt, "and ")
  #line-at(465.406pt, 284.21pt, "physical ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 308.69pt, "interpretability checks before they could serve as robust uncertainty-propagation ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 333.05pt, "coordinates. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90.024pt, 376.61pt, "4. CONCLUSIONS ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(114.02pt, 402.67pt, "This work introduces a high-fidelity, computationally efficient framework for ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 427.15pt, "uncertainty quantification in thermoreflectance inverse problems. By pairing ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 451.63pt, "normalized Monte Carlo sampling with covariance-based analytical propagation in ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 476.11pt, "physically transformed variables, we reveal two critical insights. First, Monte Carlo ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 500.47pt, "analysis is not automatically reliable when the fitted parameters differ greatly in ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 524.95pt, "magnitude: direct fitting in the original dimensional parameter space artificially ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 549.46pt, "broadens the inferred distributions and overestimates uncertainty. Fitting normalized ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 573.94pt, "parameters relative to their optimal estimates is a practical prerequisite for trustworthy ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 598.3pt, "Monte Carlo estimation. Second, we show that the multilayer heat-diffusion equation ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 622.78pt, "dictates an intrinsic statistical geometry. The approximately Gaussian variables are the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 652.06pt, "thermal resistance ")
